<Project>

  <!--
    Brief T57 (#420): every NuGet package, direct and transitive, is pinned by version and content hash.
    - RestorePackagesWithLockFile: each project keeps a packages.lock.json. A full restore compares each package's
      contentHash there with the SHA-512 recorded for the package (NU1403 on a mismatch, NU1004 when the
      dependencies or runtimes changed); a no-op restore (an up-to-date obj/) checks nothing, and a package already
      in the packages folder is trusted through its stored hash. CI checks because each run starts from a fresh
      checkout and an empty packages folder: never cache obj/ or the packages folder in a workflow.
    - RestoreLockedMode under CI=true (GitHub Actions sets it for every step): every restore on CI, including the
      implicit ones of build, test and a publish for one runtime, fails instead of updating a lock file. Local restores
      may update them; commit only changes you meant (AGENTS.md, "Building and Running").
    - RuntimeIdentifiers: TswapCli is NativeAOT for the host's runtime, so its lock file has a section per runtime
      with the ILCompiler packages. The list is what release.yml publishes (the CI hosts are among them), on all
      three projects because a publish for one runtime sets it for the referenced projects too. Ordinal order, so
      the props file and the lock files stay stable. A new shipped runtime goes here and into release.yml together.
    The ILCompiler and ILLink packages come with the SDK, so global.json names the exact SDK (rollForward: disable).
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <RestorePackagesWithLockFile>true</RestorePackagesWithLockFile>
    <RestoreLockedMode Condition="'$(CI)' == 'true'">true</RestoreLockedMode>
    <RuntimeIdentifiers>linux-arm64;linux-x64;osx-arm64;win-x64</RuntimeIdentifiers>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
